The Security Council,
Recalling all its previous relevant resolutions and statements of its President concerning the Syrian Arab Republic, including resolutions 2642 (2022) and 2672 (2023),
Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of the Syrian Arab Republic and to the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations,
Reaffirming also the primary responsibility of the Syrian authorities to protect the population in Syria and to ensure the provision of humanitarian assistance,
Recalling the humanitarian principles of humanity, neutrality, impartiality and independence, and the need for all parties to respect them,
Expressing grave concern at the continuing dire humanitarian situation in Syria, including the impact of the devastating earthquake of 6 February 2023 and the ongoing displacement, food insecurity, and lack of access to basic services, and the need for a sustained and scaled-up humanitarian response,
Stressing the need for unimpeded and sustained humanitarian access to all people in need in Syria through all modalities, including cross-line and cross-border, in accordance with international humanitarian law and the humanitarian principles,
Noting the need to ensure the transparency and effectiveness of humanitarian assistance, including timely and independent monitoring of the delivery and distribution of aid, to prevent the diversion of humanitarian assistance to terrorist groups, while ensuring that such measures do not impede humanitarian access and are carried out in consultation with United Nations humanitarian agencies,
Reaffirming that the cross-border mechanism is a temporary measure to address the urgent humanitarian needs of the civilian population and should be complemented by strengthened cross-line deliveries, without prejudice to the authorization in paragraph 1 and based on humanitarian needs,
Welcoming the efforts of the United Nations and its humanitarian partners, and noting the cooperation of the Syrian authorities in facilitating humanitarian access, and calling upon all parties to facilitate rapid, safe and unhindered humanitarian access to all areas of Syria,
Noting the reports of the Secretary-General on the humanitarian situation in Syria,
Recalling resolution 2254 (2015) and reaffirming that humanitarian assistance should not prejudge the political process,
Noting the adverse impact of unilateral coercive measures on the humanitarian situation and stressing the need to ensure that such measures do not impede the humanitarian response,
Determining that the situation in Syria continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security,
Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,
1. Decides to renew until 10 July 2024 the authorization for United Nations humanitarian agencies and their implementing partners to use the Bab al-Hawa crossing and, as needed and in consultation with the Syrian authorities, the Bab al-Salam and Al-Rai crossings for the delivery of humanitarian assistance into Syria, as set out in the relevant provisions of resolutions 2642 (2022) and 2672 (2023);
2. Requests the Secretary-General to continue to report to the Security Council on a monthly basis on the humanitarian situation in Syria, including on the implementation of this resolution, the use of all authorized crossings, the effectiveness of cross-line deliveries, early recovery projects, the needs assessment process, and the measures taken to ensure the transparency and monitoring of humanitarian assistance and to prevent its diversion, including on impediments to access, the number of beneficiaries reached, and the views of humanitarian actors, in full respect of humanitarian principles and without impeding humanitarian access, and in a manner that respects the sovereignty of Syria and does not politicize humanitarian assistance;
3. Calls upon all parties to the conflict to ensure safe, unhindered and sustained access for humanitarian actors to all areas of Syria, including those not under government control, through cross-line and cross-border modalities, in accordance with international humanitarian law and the humanitarian principles;
4. Demands that all parties comply with their obligations under international humanitarian law, including the protection of civilians and civilian objects, and refrain from any action that may impede the delivery of humanitarian assistance;
5. Requests the Secretary-General, in consultation with the Syrian Government and relevant United Nations agencies, to include in his reports an assessment of cross-line deliveries and recommendations for scaling up cross-line assistance, including to north-west Syria and the Rukban camp, with a view to an orderly transition to cross-line delivery as the main channel, based on humanitarian needs;
6. Calls upon all Member States to ensure that unilateral coercive measures do not impede humanitarian access and to consider easing such measures where they exacerbate the humanitarian situation;
7. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Council no later than six months after the adoption of this resolution on the implementation of this resolution, including on humanitarian needs, the effectiveness of cross-line deliveries, and progress in scaling up cross-line assistance, and expresses its intention to review the implementation of this resolution, without prejudice to the authorization in paragraph 1, including with a view to an orderly transition to cross-line delivery as the main channel;
8. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

The Security Council,
Recalling all its previous relevant resolutions and statements of its President concerning the Syrian Arab Republic, including resolutions 2642 (2022) and 2672 (2023),
