4. Demands that all parties comply with their obligations under international humanitarian law, including the protection of civilians and civilian objects, and refrain from any action that may impede the delivery of humanitarian assistance;
5. Requests the Secretary-General to include in his reports an assessment of cross-line deliveries and recommendations for scaling up cross-line assistance, including to north-west Syria and the Rukban camp, based on humanitarian needs and in full respect of humanitarian principles; and stresses that cross-line deliveries should complement, and not substitute for, the authorization in paragraph 1;
6. Requests the Secretary-General, in consultation with the Syrian Government and relevant United Nations agencies, to scale up early recovery projects, including the restoration of electricity, water, sanitation, health and education facilities, and demining, in accordance with humanitarian principles and respecting the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of Syria, and to report on progress in this regard;
7. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Council no later than six months after the adoption of this resolution on the implementation of this resolution, including on humanitarian needs, the effectiveness of cross-line deliveries, and progress in scaling up cross-line assistance, and reaffirms that the authorization in paragraph 1 remains in effect until 10 July 2024, and expresses its intention to review implementation on the basis of humanitarian needs and without prejudice to that authorization;
8. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

